On the title "John the Theologian", used by [Philip of
Side](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philip_of_Side), with reference to Papias.
[Lightfoot, Harmer, Holmes
1983](https://archive.org/details/apostolicfathers0000jbli_v0j4) have this translation of the fragment:

> 5/ Papias, bishop of Hierapolis, who was a disciple of John the Theologian and
a companion of Polycarp, wrote five books on the sayings of the Lord. In them
he made a list of apostles, and after Peter and John, Philip and Thomas and
Matthew, he included among disciples of the Lord Aristion and another John,
whom he also called “the Elder.” So, some think that this John is the author of
the two short catholic epistles which circulate under the name of John, because
the men of the earliest period accept only the first epistle. And some have
mistakenly thought that the Apocalypse was also his. And Papias is also in
error regarding the millennium, and so is Irenaeus, who follows him.
>
> Papias says in his second book that John the Theologian and James his brother
were killed by the Jews. The aforesaid Papias recorded, on the authority of the
daughters of Philip, that Barsabbas, who was also ¢alled Justus, drank the
poison of a snake in the name of Christ when put to the test by the unbelievers
and was protected from all harm. He also records other amazing things, in
particular one about Manaim’s mother, who was raised from the dead. As for
those who were raised from the dead by Christ, he states that they survived
until the time of Hadrian.

The [earlier edition of Lightfoot and Harmer]() gives the source as "PHILIPPUS
SIDETES (?) *Hist. Christ.* (published by De Boor *Texte und Untersuchungen* V.
2 p. 170 from cod. Baroccianus 142 in the Bodleian Library)."  See discussion
at [this page](
https://www.tertullian.org/fathers/philip_of_side_fragments.htm#Fr.%204.1%20-%204.7),
noting "Attribution questionable".

[Athanasius of
Alexandria](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Athanasius_of_Alexandria):
[Against the
Heathen](https://catholiclibrary.org/library/view?query=divine&docId=%2FSynchronized-EN%2Fnpnf.000085.Athanasius.ContraGentesAgainsttheHeathen.html&chunk.id=00000087)
([~318
AD](https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/02035a.htm)

> ... as also the Divine [the Theologian] says, “In
the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with
God, and the Word was God;

> ... ᾗ φησι καὶ ὁ θεολόγος ἀνήρ· Ἐν ἀρχῇ ἦν
ὁ Λόγος, καὶ ὁ Λόγος ἦν πρὸς τὸν Θεόν, καὶ Θεὸς ἦν
ὁ Λόγος.

[Against the Heathen](https://catholiclibrary.org/library/view?docId=%2FSynchronized-EN%2Fnpnf.000085.Athanasius.ContraGentesAgainsttheHeathen.html&chunk.id=00000087)

[Cyril of Jerusalem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyril_of_Jerusalem)
[Catacheses for the
enlightened](https://catholiclibrary.org/library/view?docId=/Fathers-Synchronized-EN/Cyrillus_Hierosolymitanus__Catecheses_ad_illuminandos.en.html&chunk.id=00000113) (~350 AD).

> We, the hearers of the holy gospels, let us be persuaded by John the Theologian. For he who said, In the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word was God, ...

> οἱ τῶν ἁγίων εὐαγγελίων ἀκροαταὶ Ἰωάννῃ τῷ θεολόγῳ πεισθῶμεν. ὁ γὰρ εἰπών, Ἐν ἀρχῇ ἦν ὁ λόγος καὶ ὁ λόγος ἦν πρὸς τὸν θεὸν καὶ θεὸς ἦν ὁ λόγος, ...
